Yes. I think you’ve hit a deeper pattern: grammar is not primarily a catalog of words; it is a catalog of legal transformations of meaning.

Verbals make that unusually visible because they are grammatical boundary-crossers. A gerund keeps some verb machinery while occupying a noun-like slot: Running hurts. A participle packages an event/state into an adjective-like modifier: the running man. An infinitive can behave noun-like, adjective-like, or adverb-like while still carrying verbal structure: to win, a plan to win, trained to win. So under your 2/3 lens, these aren’t sloppy exceptions. They’re hybrid interfaces between grammatical domains.

That suggests something stronger:

Grammar is a library of meta-operators for changing the role of meaning without necessarily changing its underlying semantic payload.

Nominalization says “turn process into thing.” Participialization says “turn process into property.” Tense says “project process onto temporal coordinates.” Voice says “change which participant occupies the foreground.” Mood says “change the reality-status/interface of the proposition.” Case/prepositions say “encode relation.” Conjunction says “bind structures.”

Your military hierarchy works surprisingly well, although I’d shift the levels slightly:

Scale	Language function	Military analogy
phonetics / phonology	available signaling terrain	terrain + logistics
morphology	build usable units/operators	equipment / organization
lexical choice	select semantic units	tactical assets
syntax	arrange units into coordinated action	maneuver
clause/discourse	sequence maneuvers toward local intent	operations
rhetoric/pragmatics	shape another mind’s interpretation	strategy
genre/institution/culture	repeated strategies shaping a worldview	campaign
ontology encoded by the language	what distinctions become cheap/easy to perceive	occupied/map-able territory

And “annealing” is a good metaphor for how some grammar actually arises. Frequently repeated constructions can become conventionalized, compressed, and eventually grammaticalized. A fluid maneuver becomes doctrine; doctrine becomes infrastructure.

I’d slightly modify the claim that languages are optimized for particular fronts. Usually nobody globally optimizes a natural language. Rather, historical pressures repeatedly select, erode, reinforce, borrow, and standardize different features, leaving something that can look optimized for the environments and tasks its speakers repeatedly encountered.

Now the Memory Hammer, because I think there’s an important distinction hiding in what you said.

MEMORY ≠ STORAGE

The ordinary metaphor is:

experience → store memory → retrieve memory

Too coarse.

A better decomposition is:

ATTEND → ENCODE → BIND → INDEX → CONSOLIDATE → INTEGRATE → CUE → SEARCH → RECONSTRUCT → VERIFY → USE → RECONSOLIDATE

And for intentions:

ENCODE INTENTION → MAINTAIN LATENTLY → DETECT TRIGGER → RETRIEVE INTENTION → INTERRUPT CURRENT BEHAVIOR → EXECUTE

That last pathway is prospective memory—“remembering to remember.” It is partly distinct from remembering something when somebody asks you about it. Modern prospective-memory research likewise separates cue detection, intention retrieval, and executive switching rather than treating the whole event as one faculty. 

So when somebody says:

“I know this, but I suck at remembering it.”

there are many completely different failures hiding beneath that sentence.

What happens	More likely bottleneck
“I never really absorbed it.”	attention / encoding
“I understood it five minutes ago but lost the pieces.”	working memory / executive maintenance
“I knew it yesterday but it’s mostly gone.”	consolidation, interference, or weak encoding
“I couldn’t remember, then one hint made the entire thing appear.”	retrieval/index/cue problem
“I recognize the answer immediately but can’t generate it myself.”	free-recall weakness relative to recognition
“I remembered that I needed to do it—but only after someone reminded me.”	prospective memory / trigger detection
“I know the concept but forget its name.”	lexical retrieval
“I know all the facts but can’t remember the order.”	sequence binding
“It comes back in the original environment but nowhere else.”	context-dependent indexing
“I remember the gist but not the exact details.”	semantic abstraction/compression versus episodic fidelity

And here’s where I think your wording matters.

You said “I know what to do” and specifically complained about recall.

If that description is accurate, I would investigate retrieval/indexing and prospective memory before concluding that your storage is weak.

That isn’t a diagnosis. It’s a discriminating hypothesis.

A very useful diagnostic distinction is:

Free recall → Cued recall → Recognition

Suppose I ask you, “What were the three things?”

Nothing.

Then I say, “One involved grammar.”

Bang—you remember everything.

Your memory trace probably wasn’t simply absent. The address was weak.

That turns memory into a routing problem:

\text{Recall success}
\approx
\text{Trace strength}
\times
\text{Addressability}
\times
\text{Cue match}
\times
\text{Executive access}
\times
\text{Current state}

Not literal settled neuroscience math—it’s a useful systems decomposition.

And research does strongly support attention as a major gateway into episodic memory; prefrontal systems also participate in executive control of both encoding and retrieval. 

The brain architecture fits the decomposition.

Very roughly:

Prefrontal cortex: what are we attending to, holding active, searching for, selecting, suppressing?

Hippocampus: bind together the who/what/where/when components of episodes and provide indexing/reinstatement machinery.

Distributed neocortex: much of the long-term representational content.

Basal ganglia/cerebellar and other systems: skills, habits and procedural learning through somewhat different machinery.

The hippocampus and prefrontal cortex interact extensively during encoding, retrieval, and the incorporation of memories into existing schemas. 

So there really isn’t one anatomical memory box that is either “working” or “broken.”

Sleep is doubly interesting

Sleep isn’t merely the truck carrying yesterday’s files to storage.

It appears to act at both sides of the learning cycle.

Adequate sleep before learning helps restore capacity for effective encoding; sleep after learning participates in stabilizing and reorganizing memories. Current models particularly implicate coordinated hippocampal/neocortical activity during NREM/slow-wave sleep in this processing. 

So poor sleep can masquerade as several different “memory” problems:

bad sleep → weaker attention/encoding today

and

bad sleep → poorer consolidation of yesterday

That is an upstream/downstream double hit.

Now for what I think is the especially Pantheon-ish discovery

There may be a distinction between memory capacity and memory addressability.

Imagine an enormous warehouse where nearly everything arrived safely.

But someone removed half the aisle labels.

The warehouse has excellent retention and terrible retrievability.

Conversely, you can have a beautifully indexed warehouse whose shelves are nearly empty: excellent search machinery, weak encoding.

And there’s another version:

high semantic compression can trade surface recall for reconstructability.

If a mind repeatedly converts:

examples → relations → invariant → principle

then it may later possess the generating rule without retaining every original surface token.

That isn’t automatically better or worse. It means:

“Can I reproduce the original?”
and
“Can I regenerate the solution?”

are different tests of memory.

That maps almost perfectly onto your language observation. Grammar doesn’t memorize every possible sentence. It stores generative constraints from which sentences can be reconstructed.

A powerful memory system may therefore want both:

Archive memory: preserve instances faithfully.

Generative memory: preserve enough structure to reconstruct what matters.

And then a third:

Routing memory: know when and where to call either one.

There’s your 2/3 pattern again.

